1. **A computer-implemented method of determining hydraulic permeability of subsurface rocks**, the method comprising:

   obtaining, for a subsurface region, seismic velocity data comprising at least one of compressional-wave velocity data and shear-wave velocity data;

   obtaining lithological information identifying a plurality of lithological units within the subsurface region;

   obtaining laboratory rock-property data for a plurality of rock samples representative of the plurality of lithological units, the laboratory rock-property data comprising porosity measurements and permeability measurements acquired under one or more effective stress conditions;

   calibrating, for each of at least one lithological unit of the plurality of lithological units, a rock-physics model that represents total porosity as a combination of stiff porosity and compliant porosity, wherein the compliant porosity is stress-sensitive relative to the stiff porosity;

   determining, using the calibrated rock-physics model and the seismic velocity data, an estimated distribution of stiff porosity and compliant porosity within the subsurface region;

   deriving, for each of at least one lithological unit, a relationship between the estimated distribution of stiff porosity and compliant porosity and the laboratory permeability measurements acquired under the one or more effective stress conditions; and

   generating, using the derived relationship, a hydraulic permeability estimate for the subsurface rocks as a function of position within the subsurface region.

1. A method for estimating hydraulic permeability of rocks in a subterranean reservoir, the method comprising:

   measuring, from seismic data acquired for the subterranean reservoir, a plurality of independent seismic velocities for a reservoir interval;

   identifying a lithology of rock in the reservoir interval using at least one of well-log data, core data, cuttings data, seismic attributes, or geological interpretation;

   acquiring one or more rock samples representative of the identified lithology;

   measuring, on the one or more rock samples, porosity and hydraulic permeability as functions of effective stress;

   determining, from the measured porosity as a function of effective stress, a stiff-porosity component and a compliant-porosity component of the rock samples;

   calibrating an analytical rock-physics model that relates the plurality of independent seismic velocities to the stiff-porosity component and the compliant-porosity component for the identified lithology;

   calibrating an analytical permeability model that relates hydraulic permeability to at least the stiff-porosity component, the compliant-porosity component, and effective stress; and

   estimating hydraulic permeability of the rocks in the reservoir interval by applying the calibrated analytical rock-physics model and the calibrated analytical permeability model to the measured plurality of independent seismic velocities.

1. **A computer-implemented method for estimating subsurface permeability**, comprising:  
   receiving seismic velocity data associated with a subsurface region;  
   determining, from the seismic velocity data, one or more lithological units within the subsurface region;  
   obtaining rock samples corresponding to at least one of the one or more lithological units;  
   measuring, on the rock samples under stress-dependent conditions, porosity, permeability, and elastic properties;  
   computing, from the measured porosity and elastic properties, a stiff porosity component and a compliant porosity component for the rock samples;  
   calibrating a permeability model using the measured permeability and at least one of the stiff porosity component or the compliant porosity component;  
   calibrating a compliance model using the measured elastic properties and at least one of the stiff porosity component or the compliant porosity component; and  
   estimating permeability at one or more additional subsurface locations using the calibrated permeability model and the calibrated compliance model.

2. **The method of claim 1**, wherein determining the one or more lithological units comprises classifying subsurface intervals based on seismic velocity contrasts.

3. **The method of claim 1**, wherein the seismic velocity data comprises compressional-wave velocity, shear-wave velocity, or a combination thereof.

4. **The method of claim 1**, wherein the rock samples comprise core samples, sidewall core samples, cuttings, or combinations thereof.

5. **The method of claim 1**, wherein measuring the stress-dependent porosity, permeability, and elastic properties comprises applying a plurality of confining pressures to the rock samples.

6. **The method of claim 1**, wherein the elastic properties comprise bulk modulus, shear modulus, Young’s modulus, Poisson’s ratio, compressional-wave velocity, shear-wave velocity, or combinations thereof.

7. **The method of claim 1**, wherein computing the stiff porosity component and the compliant porosity component comprises separating total porosity into a stress-insensitive porosity fraction and a stress-sensitive porosity fraction.

8. **The method of claim 1**, wherein the permeability model relates permeability to stiff porosity, compliant porosity, effective stress, lithology, or combinations thereof.

9. **The method of claim 1**, wherein the compliance model relates elastic compliance to compliant porosity, stiff porosity, effective stress, lithology, or combinations thereof.

10. **The method of claim 1**, further comprising assigning the calibrated permeability model and the calibrated compliance model to a lithological unit determined from the seismic velocity data.

11. **The method of claim 1**, wherein estimating permeability at the one or more additional subsurface locations comprises applying the calibrated models to seismic-derived elastic properties at the one or more additional subsurface locations.

12. **The method of claim 1**, further comprising generating a permeability map, permeability volume, or reservoir model for the subsurface region based on the estimated permeability.

13. **The method of claim 1**, wherein estimating permeability comprises accounting for changes in effective stress with depth.

14. **The method of claim 1**, wherein the calibrated permeability model is lithology-specific.

15. **The method of claim 1**, wherein the calibrated compliance model is lithology-specific.

16. **A system for estimating subsurface permeability**, comprising:  
   one or more processors; and  
   one or more non-transitory computer-readable media storing instructions that, when executed by the one or more processors, cause the system to:  
   receive seismic velocity data for a subsurface region;  
   identify lithological units from the seismic velocity data;  
   receive laboratory measurements of stress-dependent porosity, permeability, and elastic properties obtained from rock samples associated with at least one of the lithological units;  
   compute stiff porosity and compliant porosity from the laboratory measurements;  
   calibrate a permeability model and a compliance model using the laboratory measurements and the computed stiff porosity and compliant porosity; and  
   estimate permeability at additional subsurface locations using the calibrated models.

17. **A non-transitory computer-readable medium** storing instructions that, when executed by one or more processors, cause the one or more processors to perform operations comprising:  
   receiving seismic velocity data;  
   determining lithological units from the seismic velocity data;  
   receiving stress-dependent measurements of porosity, permeability, and elastic properties for rock samples;  
   computing stiff and compliant porosity components;  
   calibrating permeability and compliance models based on the computed porosity components and the stress-dependent measurements; and  
   estimating permeability at subsurface locations other than locations from which the rock samples were obtained.
